# examples/chime overlay fragment for the Raspberry Pi boards: bring back the
# ALSA core GoSD's stock kernels cut, with *only* the VideoCore firmware audio
# driver (snd_bcm2835) compiled in. That driver routes PCM to HDMI (and, on
# boards that have one, the analog jack) through the mailbox/VCHIQ firmware
# interface, so audio needs no DRM and no ASoC here — merged after
# build/boards/<board>/kernel.fragment (overlay wins).
#
# CONFIG_BCM2835_VCHIQ and CONFIG_RASPBERRYPI_FIRMWARE are already enabled in
# every stock GoSD Pi kernel, so this fragment adds the sound core and nothing
# else. The recipe's DTS-style patch (patches/) defaults the driver's HDMI
# audio device to on, which a Kconfig fragment cannot do portably — see the
# patch's own header for why.
CONFIG_SOUND=y
CONFIG_SND=y
CONFIG_SND_BCM2835=y

# Everything below is a deny-list, and it is the load-bearing half of this
# file. The raspberrypi defconfigs ship the whole Pi audio ecosystem as
# modules, and GoSD kernels are monolithic (CONFIG_MODULES is always off), so
# `make olddefconfig` promotes every one of those to built-in the moment
# CONFIG_SND is enabled. Left unstated, a three-line "enable sound" fragment
# silently compiles in ~60 HAT machine drivers (HiFiBerry, IQaudio, JustBoom,
# Allo, AudioInjector, Pisound...), every ASoC codec they reference, USB audio,
# the MIDI sequencer and OSS emulation — measured on the sattrack fragment,
# whose comment claiming "no codec, machine, or USB audio drivers come with it"
# is simply wrong (bean gosd-df57 records the numbers).
#
# ASoC is the big one: SND_SOC gates every codec and machine driver above, and
# snd_bcm2835 is a plain ALSA driver that does not need it.
# CONFIG_SND_SOC is not set

# Generic ALSA driver menus: dummy/loopback/MIDI-interface cards we have no
# hardware for.
# CONFIG_SND_DRIVERS is not set
# CONFIG_SND_ARM is not set
# CONFIG_SND_SPI is not set
# CONFIG_SND_USB is not set

# No MIDI, no sequencer, no OSS emulation, no high-res-timer sound clock: this
# appliance plays PCM out of one device node.
# CONFIG_SND_SEQUENCER is not set
# CONFIG_SND_OSSEMUL is not set
# CONFIG_SND_HRTIMER is not set
# CONFIG_SND_SUPPORT_OLD_API is not set

# The two lines below are not about sound at all, and they are why a first
# build of this fragment was thrown away. GoSD's stock Pi fragments enable the
# USB gadget stack, and the defconfig's USB MIDI gadget function sits dormant
# in there because it depends on the raw-MIDI core, which cannot exist while
# CONFIG_SOUND is off. Turn sound on and the *legacy* MIDI gadget driver wakes
# up with it, along with that raw-MIDI core — none of which appear in a stock
# GoSD kernel.config. Legacy gadget drivers claim the board's only UDC at
# probe, which is exactly how bean gosd-spjt's "Gadget Zero" bug stopped
# --usb-gadget working, so an app that wants audio AND a USB gadget needs these
# off. (Note for anyone editing this file: don't write a symbol name followed
# by an equals sign in a comment here. merge_config.sh pattern-matches the
# whole fragment, comments included, and reports the comment as a redefinition
# — confusing, though it does pick the real line.)
# CONFIG_USB_MIDI_GADGET is not set
# CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_F_MIDI is not set
